@ 17.8.6 - Proto-Indo-European to Doric Greek
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Tucker, R. Whitney (1969), “Chronology of Greek Sound Changes”. The American Journal of Philology 90(1):36 – 47; and Wikipedia contributors (2013), “Ancient Greek dialects”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. Vowel contraction (on which the author does not elaborate much)
	{[+laryngeal],m̩,n̩} > a
	{ bʱ, dʱ, ɡʱ } > { pʰ, tʰ, kʰ }
	s > h / #_
	s > h / V_V
	t > t͡s / _i
	j > h
	{ kʷ, kʰʷ, ɡʷ } > { t, tʰ, d } / _:[+front]
	{ kʷ, kʰʷ, ɡʷ } > { p, pʰ, b } / :{ _a, _o, _C }:
	{ kʷ, kʰʷ, ɡʷ } > { k, kʰ, ɡ } / _u
	{ kʷ, kʰʷ, ɡʷ } > { k, kʰ, ɡ } / u_
	t͡s > s
	h > ∅
	Vns > { V, V:[+long] }s (P:[-voice]ucker says that “:[i]n a few P:[+voice]oric dialect͡s the lengthening did not occur”)
	n > ∅ / _s
	t͡ʃ > t / #_
	t͡ʃ > t:[+long] / medial
	h > ∅ (in those “dialect͡s of the western fringe of:[+affricate]sia Minor and the near-by islands”)